  {"type": "split", "h2": "Electric furnace vs heat pump with air handler: the honest math", "image": "Midea EVOX G3 electric air handler", "image_src": "/assets/img/brands/midea-evox-g3-air-handler.webp", "image_fit": "contain", "image_pos": "50% 50%", "image_credit": "Image: Midea", "html": "<p>An <strong>electric furnace</strong> makes heat with resistance coils, like a giant toaster. It's simple, reliable and 100% efficient at the point of use, but every unit of heat costs a full unit of electricity. On Ontario hydro rates, that makes it the most expensive way to heat a whole house.</p><p>A <strong>heat pump with an electric air handler</strong> moves heat from outside instead of making it. A cold-climate unit like the Midea EVOX G3 delivers two to three units of heat per unit of electricity, even in cold weather, and still has electric backup strips in the air handler for the coldest nights. Same ducts, same 240V panel work, a fraction of the running cost.</p><p>That's why, for most Ontario homes without gas, we recommend the heat pump and air handler. It also qualifies for the higher rebate tier, since the home is heated by electricity. We'll install a straight electric furnace when the budget or the property calls for it, and we'll say which is which.</p>"},
